## P2b — Microsoft Entra authentication to Azure Database for PostgreSQL

Context: Before this change nothing in OntoBricks could connect to a generic
PostgreSQL server. `LakebaseAuth` only knew how to mint Lakebase JWTs, resolving the host
by walking the Databricks Postgres control plane (projects → branches → endpoints), so an
Azure server was unreachable regardless of configuration.

Rather than rewrite the connection layer, this reuses it. `LakebaseConnectionPool` already
expects only `host` / `port` / `database` / `user` / `password()` / `invalidate()` /
`kwargs()` from its auth object, and it already retries once on an authentication failure
after calling `invalidate()`. That loop is precisely what Entra token rotation needs, so a
sibling auth class drops straight in — 1050 LOC of pool machinery kept, ~330 LOC of new
credential code added.

Changes:

1. src/back/core/postgres/EntraCredential.py (new)
   Mints and caches Entra access tokens for the
   `https://ossrdbms-aad.database.windows.net/.default` audience. Re-mints 300s before
   expiry, because a token that expires mid-handshake fails the connection outright.
   Thread-safe — the pool opens connections from several threads and a token stampede
   would be both slow and rate-limited. `DefaultAzureCredential` is constructed lazily so
   importing the module never requires Azure configuration. Credential failures become
   `InfrastructureError` naming both remedies (managed identity in a container,
   `az login` locally) rather than surfacing a vendor exception.
2. src/back/core/postgres/PostgresAuth.py (new)
   Same surface as `LakebaseAuth`, minus the Lakebase-specific parts: no project/branch/
   endpoint discovery, no JWT. Coordinates come from standard `PG*` variables; the
   password is an Entra token or `PGPASSWORD`. `sslmode` defaults to `require` (Azure
   mandates TLS). Missing coordinates raise `ValidationError` naming the variable and, for
   `PGUSER`, explaining that Entra expects the principal name mapped via
   `pgaadauth_create_principal`. TCP keepalives carried over from `LakebaseAuth`, since
   without them a dead pooled connection stalls the next query for ~130s.
3. src/back/core/databricks/lakebase/LakebaseAuth.py
   `resolve_pg_auth_mode()` + a reworked `get_lakebase_auth()` now select the
   implementation. Explicit `ONTOBRICKS_PG_AUTH` (`lakebase` | `entra` | `password`) wins;
   otherwise a `*.postgres.database.azure.com` host infers Entra, and everything else
   keeps the previous Lakebase behaviour — so **existing deployments are unaffected**.
   The singleton is now keyed by resolved mode, so a mode change no longer hands back a
   stale object.
4. tests/units/postgres/test_entra_credential.py (new, 8 tests)
   Scope value, caching, re-mint inside the refresh margin, `invalidate()` forcing a fresh
   token, and failures mapping to `InfrastructureError`. One test initially asserted that
   a whitespace-only token was accepted — the implementation was wrong, not the test name:
   `" "` is truthy so it slipped past the guard and would have reached libpq as an opaque
   auth failure. `token()` now strips before checking, and the test asserts rejection.
5. tests/units/postgres/test_postgres_auth.py (new, 20 tests)
   Mode selection and rejection of unknown modes, `kwargs()` shape, TLS default and
   tightening, keepalives, each missing coordinate named, non-numeric `PGPORT`, both
   password paths, and `invalidate()` being safe in password mode. Four selector tests pin
   that non-Azure and unset hosts still resolve to `lakebase`.
6. tests/units/postgres/test_entra_live_connection.py (new, 3 tests)
   The unit tests stub the credential, so none of them prove Azure's real token is
   accepted. These do, against the live server: a real token authenticates; the shared
   pool drives `password()` per physical connection (the property the whole design rests
   on); and invalidate-then-reconnect recovers. Gated on `ONTOBRICKS_ENTRA_LIVE=1`.
   The coordinates are snapshotted at *module import*, because `tests/conftest.py` has an
   autouse fixture that deletes `PGHOST`/`PGDATABASE` to stop a developer's `.env` leaking
   into unit tests — a safeguard worth keeping, so this module works around it rather than
   weakening it.
7. .env.example
   Documented the Azure path as Option 1 (with the one-time
   `pgaadauth_create_principal` / `CREATE SCHEMA` / `GRANT` setup and the co-tenancy
   guarantee) and demoted Lakebase to Option 2.

