## P5b — remove Lakeflow managed-synced mode

Context: `managed_synced` delegated bulk R2RML data movement to a Databricks Lakeflow
synced-table pipeline. It is Databricks-data-plane-only and has no equivalent on a generic
PostgreSQL server, so it cannot survive the decoupling. `app_managed` — the app streaming
warehouse rows into Postgres — is now the only path.

**Design decision: the three-object layout stays.** Each graph version remains
`*_sync` (bulk) + `*__app` (companion) + `*` (union view), even though both tables are now
app-written. The bulk/derived split is what lets a rebuild replace bulk triples without
discarding inferred ones, and collapsing it would force a schema migration on every
existing deployment. The `_sync` suffix is now historical; that is documented where it
matters rather than renamed, since renaming physical tables would need the same migration.

**Design decision: legacy `sync_*` config keys are tolerated, not rejected.** Existing
stored `graph_engine_config` blobs carry `sync_mode`, `sync_table_mode`, `sync_timeout_s`,
`sync_uc_catalog`, `sync_uc_schema`. Rejecting them in `validate_engine_config_keys` would
make a saved config fail validation and block Save in the Settings UI, so they are accepted
and logged at debug. The Settings JS *deletes* them on save, so configs clean themselves up
as admins touch them.

**Design decision: `build_runs.sync_mode` column is kept.** It records what mode a
historical build used. Dropping it needs a migration, and it is harmless; new rows write
`""`.

